{#
	This grid is read from across an office, on a wall monitor nobody walks up to. Everything
	has to fit on one screen next to the log list, so each project is a single dense row rather
	than a card, and the column count keeps climbing with the viewport.

	Each row is a 12-column grid and every status component brings its own span, because the
	component list is configurable through bug_catcher.status_list_components - the row cannot
	know what it will be asked to contain.

	A project with performance switched on gets a different list, and which one depends on its
	workload: bug_catcher.perf_status_list_components for a web project, and
	bug_catcher.worker_status_list_components for a cron box, where latency measures nothing
	because nobody is waiting. StatusList::componentsFor() is where both questions are asked.

	`dense` is read once for the whole wall, not per row: all three lists cut the same twelve
	columns differently, and a wall of cards is read by scanning down a column. One project with
	performance on therefore puts every row on the same grid - the ones without it just get a
	wider sparkline where the two numbers would be.
#}
<div{{ attributes.defaults({
	class:'space-y-2'
}) }}>
	<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1.5 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 xl:grid-cols-4 2xl:grid-cols-5">
		{% set dense = this.dense %}
		{% for project in this.projects %}
			<div class="grid grid-cols-12 items-center gap-x-2 rounded-md border border-line bg-surface/50 px-2 py-1">
				{% for statusComponent in this.componentsFor(project) %}
					{{ component(statusComponent, {project: project, dense: dense}) }}
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
	<twig:Favicon/>
</div>
